---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { meta, upcoming } from '../../lib/data.js';
const cov = meta.coverage;
const rejected = Object.entries(meta.stats)
  .filter(([k]) => k.startsWith('rejected:'))
  .map(([k, v]) => [k.replace('rejected:', ''), v])
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base title="What's covered — happenings" description="Where happenings gets its data, what it covers, and what it misses.">
  <p class="kicker" style="margin-top:26px">happenings</p>
  <h1 class="page">What's covered</h1>
  <p class="lede">And, more usefully, what isn't. A directory that is
    confidently wrong is worse than one that admits a gap.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">Where this comes from</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px">
    <tr>
      <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding-bottom:6px">Source</th>
      <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding-bottom:6px">Rows fetched</th>
      <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding-bottom:6px">Last checked</th>
    </tr>
    {meta.sources.map((s) => (
      <tr>
        <td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3)">
          <b>{s.label}</b><br />
          <span style="color:var(--ink-2)">{s.attribution}</span>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums">
          {s.raw_rows.toLocaleString()}
        </td>
        <td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3);color:var(--ink-2)">
          {new Date(s.fetched_at).toLocaleString('en-US', { dateStyle: 'medium', timeZone: meta.tz })}
        </td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h2 class="sec">Coverage, honestly</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <div class="tiles">
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{cov.neighborhood}%</div><div class="m">placed to a neighborhood</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{cov.coordinates}%</div><div class="m">have coordinates</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{cov.url}%</div><div class="m">link to a detail page</div></div>
    <div class="tile" style="border-color:var(--pink-deep)"><div class="n">{cov.price}%</div><div class="m">state a price</div></div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px;max-width:70ch">
    <b>No source in this build states a price</b>, so there is no price filter and
    nothing is marked free. Many of these events almost certainly are free — city
    parks programming generally is — but asserting that would be guessing, and
    guessing is the one thing this index doesn't do. An event with no stated
    price is <em>unknown</em>, not free.
  </p>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">
    <b>Only {cov.neighborhood}% of events are placed to a neighborhood.</b> The
    parks feed carries coordinates; the permitted-events feed gives a street
    description and a borough, so those events can be filtered by borough but not
    placed on a block. Geocoding those descriptions is the single highest-value
    next step for this site.
  </p>

  <h2 class="sec">What gets thrown out, and why</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">Rejected rows keep their titles so the classifier can
    be audited by reading a file rather than re-deriving the evidence.</p>
  <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px">
    {rejected.map(([reason, n]) => (
      <tr>
        <td style="padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;width:90px">
          <b>{n.toLocaleString()}</b>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3)">{reason}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p style="max-width:70ch;margin-top:14px">
    The big one is league field permits. The city's permitted-events feed is
    mostly youth and adult sports bookings — real permits, but not things you can
    turn up to, so they are quarantined rather than listed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="sec">What's missing</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <ul style="max-width:70ch;line-height:1.65">
    <li><b>Ticketed venues.</b> Pioneer Works, Roulette, Le Poisson Rouge, the
      university lecture calendars — none are here yet, and they are the whole
      reason the project exists.</li>
    <li><b>Photos for events.</b> No event feed here carries one. The
      <a href="/nyc/places">places</a> do, via Wikimedia Commons — everything
      else gets its category block.</li>
    <li><b>The last 30% of places.</b> 518 location strings, mostly street
      intersections, that no park name matched and no geocoder resolved
      confidently. They are ranked by how many events ride on them.</li>
    <li><b>Prices and ticket links.</b> Needs sources that publish them.</li>
    <li><b>Subway filtering.</b> Designed, not built. Stations don't move, so
      it's a one-time static join against GTFS.</li>
    <li><b>Recurring series.</b> The regular Tuesday thing is the most useful
      entity in the city and it isn't detected yet.</li>
    <li><b>Neighborhood "reach".</b> Nobody lives inside a polygon — an edition
      should show what's nearby, weighted by how hard it is to get there, not
      just what falls inside an outline.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="sec">Ground rules</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <ul style="max-width:70ch;line-height:1.65">
    <li>Only endpoints published for everyone. No login-gated data, no CAPTCHA
      solving, no proxy rotation.</li>
    <li><code>robots.txt</code> honoured, requests paced and self-identifying,
      <code>Retry-After</code> respected.</li>
    <li>Facts only — title, time, place, category. Every listing links back to
      its source.</li>
    <li>Nothing is estimated. Missing shows as missing.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
